INT. DEEP-SEA RESEARCH STATION "ABYSSAL ONE" - OBSERVATION DOME - NIGHT

A glass bubble on the ocean floor, three thousand meters down. Beyond the curved glass: black water, drifting snow, a lamp throwing a lonely cone. Pipes sweat. A kettle ticks on a hot plate.

DR. ARUNDHATI SENGUPTA, 50s, a thermal sweater and wire-rimmed glasses, sits cross-legged on the deck with a thermos. LIEUTENANT KAVEH MORTAZAVI, 30s, in a naval jumpsuit, stands at the glass, one palm flat against it.

KAVEH
Did you see that?

SENGUPTA
See what?

KAVEH
Something moved.

SENGUPTA
Something always moves, Lieutenant. It's the deep sea.

KAVEH
Not like that.

SENGUPTA
Describe it.

KAVEH
Big. Slow. Pale. It went across the lamp and then, nothing.

SENGUPTA
(to herself)
Anglerfish. Or a squid. Or the reflection of your own eyelid.

KAVEH
It was not my eyelid.

SENGUPTA
Then it was a sperm whale.

KAVEH
Not at this depth.

SENGUPTA
No.

She unscrews the thermos. Pours. Offers him a cup. He doesn't take it.

SENGUPTA (CONT'D)
Kaveh. When did you last sleep?

KAVEH
I don't remember.

SENGUPTA
Thirty-one hours. I counted.

KAVEH
You counted?

SENGUPTA
I count everything. It's a scientific disorder.

He almost laughs. Then the lamp flickers. The water beyond the glass shifts and the snow swirls into a vortex. Kaveh steps back.

KAVEH
There. There it is.

Sengupta rises. Joins him. A vast pale shape, a flank, a curve, slides across the glass, brushing it, so close its skin leaves a smear in the silt. For a long moment the two of them simply stare.

SENGUPTA
(whispering)
Oh.

KAVEH
What is it?

SENGUPTA
(to herself)
I have no name for it.

KAVEH
You always have a name.

SENGUPTA
Not for this.

The shape rolls. An eye the size of a dinner plate regards them through the glass, wet and black, ancient, unhurried.

KAVEH
It's looking at us.

SENGUPTA
Yes.

KAVEH
Is it dangerous?

SENGUPTA
(softly)
I think it's curious.

KAVEH
Is that better?

SENGUPTA
It depends on what it's curious about.

A low hum builds in the station walls, a vibration they feel in their teeth. The kettle rattles on the hot plate. The creature presses a flipper against the dome, and the glass creaks.

KAVEH
(low)
Doctor.

SENGUPTA
I know.

KAVEH
That's a stress fracture.

SENGUPTA
I know.

KAVEH
We should seal the dome.

SENGUPTA
No. Wait.

She lifts one hand and, slowly, places her palm on the glass opposite the creature's flipper. The hum falls. The creak stops. The great eye blinks, and the creature drifts back, and back, until it is only a pale suggestion in the dark.

Kaveh lets out a breath that fogs the glass. Sengupta lowers her hand, picks up the thermos, and pours herself a cup that shakes in her grip.

KAVEH
You were right.

SENGUPTA
About what?

KAVEH
Curious.

SENGUPTA
(smiling, tired)
I do hate being right at depth.
